{
  "schema_version": "1.0",
  "article": {
    "id": "re-foundations-7b-seeing-the-impossible",
    "title": "RE Foundations / 7B / Seeing the Impossible",
    "text_type": "informational",
    "audience": {
      "grade_band": "upper_elementary"
    },
    "language": "en"
  },
  "content": {
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "order": 1,
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "s1",
            "text": "Can you believe everything you see?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s2",
            "text": "Not always!"
          },
          {
            "id": "s3",
            "text": "Sometimes our minds and our eyes make mistakes and get confused."
          },
          {
            "id": "s4",
            "text": "This may be because we are looking at an optical illusion."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "order": 2,
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "s5",
            "text": "The word optical means “related to sight”—the way we see things."
          },
          {
            "id": "s6",
            "text": "An illusion is something that looks different from the way it really is."
          },
          {
            "id": "s7",
            "text": "In short, an optical illusion is a trick that our eyes play on us."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "order": 3,
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "s8",
            "text": "Look at these optical illusions and compare what you see with what your classmates see."
          },
          {
            "id": "s9",
            "text": "The way we see things is often personal, so not everyone will see things the same way."
          },
          {
            "id": "s12",
            "text": "At a first look, most people say “No.”"
          },
          {
            "id": "s13",
            "text": "But if you compare the lines against an object with a straight edge, you’ll see otherwise."
          },
          {
            "id": "s14",
            "text": "The small circles in the squares help create the illusion."
          },
          {
            "id": "s15",
            "text": "2. What color are the squares?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s16",
            "text": "Square 1 is clearly gray."
          },
          {
            "id": "s17",
            "text": "But what about square 2?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s18",
            "text": "Is it white?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s19",
            "text": "Is it light gray?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s20",
            "text": "You may not believe it, but squares and 2 are exactly the same color."
          },
          {
            "id": "s21",
            "text": "Your eyes see the colors, but your brain notices the shadow made by the apple."
          },
          {
            "id": "s22",
            "text": "It therefore decides that the square in the shadow is a lighter color than it really is."
          },
          {
            "id": "s23",
            "text": "3. Are the circles moving?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s24",
            "text": "If you look closely at this picture, the circles may appear to move."
          },
          {
            "id": "s25",
            "text": "Of course, this is impossible."
          },
          {
            "id": "s26",
            "text": "How can a picture move?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s27",
            "text": "When we see circle-in-circle shapes, like in car wheels, they are usually moving."
          },
          {
            "id": "s28",
            "text": "Our brains are used to seeing these shapes move."
          },
          {
            "id": "s29",
            "text": "When our eyes see this shape, our mind decides that the image is moving."
          },
          {
            "id": "s30",
            "text": "Other scientists believe the illusion of movement is caused by the movements of our eyes as we look at the different colors and patterns of the picture."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
